<!--
  Charge (2026-11): navigate to a staging pose in front of the charger with
  move.xml's machinery, check that the charger's tag is in view, switch the
  gait to MPC, and walk the last metre onto the charger on the tag itself --
  TrackPose following the sightings PublishLandmark publishes -- ending
  there, standing on the charger in MPC.

  Selected per goal by NavigateToGoal.behavior_tree ("charge.xml"), like
  dock.xml, whose header is the design record for everything this tree
  shares with it: the staging leg (move.xml with goal="{staging_pose}"), the
  overlay clear, the MPC switch and its check (SetMotionKey 5 ->
  WaitForMotionState 5 -> Wait 3000), RL restored only on a failure after the
  switch was attempted, no lie-down (the caller's LIEDOWN step), no in-tree
  retry of the approach, the attribution and the status sentences. Keep the
  two in step. What is different here, and why:

  The goal's `pose` is the TAG, not where the robot ends: the charger's
  marker in the map, its yaw pointing out of its face (what the console's
  Charger waypoint stores from its Detect button). The map pose is only good
  enough to get in front of the charger. ComputeStagingPose facing_landmark
  puts the staging pose `distance` out along that yaw, turned to face the
  tag: 1.3 m, where the tag fills enough of the image for a pose and the
  robot has 0.76 m to walk in.

  Why not dock.xml with the tag's pose: DockToPose drives to a pose fixed in
  odom at acceptance, so it ends wherever the map said the charger was -- off
  by the localizer's error and the waypoint's, centimetres either way, on
  contacts that take about three. TrackPose (syncai_common/TrackPose,
  controller_server) drives to where the TAG is seen to be, corrected by
  every sighting, with the Charging controller (ApproachController), which
  strafes: a charger's tolerance is across its axis, and Docking's law only
  walks forward and turns.

  DetectLandmark before the gait switch: the tag has to be in view from the
  staging pose, or the approach would end LOST five seconds after an MPC
  switch nobody needed. It also wakes the detector, which sleeps until
  polled (its first answers are SENSOR_STARTING). 5 s, then DOCK_FAILED with
  the detector's last word; the gait was not touched, so nothing is
  restored.

  The last metre is a Parallel of two nodes, because the detector publishes
  nothing by itself and the controller judges nothing:
   - PublishLandmark polls detect_landmark and publishes the sightings it
     TRUSTS on tracked_landmark -- not ambiguous (ArUco's mirrored solution
     not yet ruled out), at least 3 sightings in a row. It never succeeds;
     it fails only when detection cannot work at all (unknown landmark, the
     detector silent or SENSOR_UNAVAILABLE for 5 s). A tag out of view is
     not its failure: nothing is published, and TrackPose's pose_timeout
     (stop) and lost_timeout (LOST) judge that.
   - TrackPose follows that topic. Its success ends the Parallel
     (success_threshold 1) and halts the publisher; either one failing
     fails it (failure_threshold 1), which cancels the approach.
  Dropping every ambiguous sighting is the first setting, not a finding: the
  ambiguity is worst near the tag's axis at about a metre -- the staging
  pose -- where the two solutions also nearly coincide. PublishLandmark logs
  how many it dropped when it ends; if the approach starves there, set
  accept_ambiguous and let track_pose's own test (targets that scatter in
  odom stop the robot) hold the flips.

  THE CHARGER'S GEOMETRY IS THE TrackPose offset AND NOWHERE ELSE: offset_x
  is where base_link stands on the charger, metres out from the tag along
  its face normal, and offset_yaw pi turns the robot to face it. 0.54 =
  SyncAI-Robot-Charger's stop, 0.27 m from the tag at the CAMERA, plus the
  camera's 0.27 m ahead of base_link (G23.urdf). Derived, not measured: lay
  the robot on the charger and read the tag's distance in base_link. The
  Charging controller's view_distance (controller_server_params.yaml) must
  equal it -- that is the point the nose is held on, the tag.

  filter_window 1.0: track_pose drives to the median of the last second of
  sightings (taken into odom, where a charger stands still), because one
  sighting's yaw noise moves the target centimetres across its axis; the
  same window is its test that the odometry is holding still. The server
  freezes the target once the robot is inside the goal checker's
  tolerances, where the tag may leave the image.

  Leaving the charger is undock.xml, unchanged: MPC, 1 m straight back,
  blind, RL. A robot laid down on the charger is stood up by the caller
  first.

  A preempt that moves {goal} after the staging leg reaches neither node:
  TrackPose's goal has no pose in it, and the tag in view is the one driven
  to. Send a moved charger once this goal has ended.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="Charge">
      <!-- New goal, clean obstacle memory: move.xml's first node (see dock.xml's header) -->
      <ForceSuccess>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-NewGoal" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay" server_timeout="1000"/>
      </ForceSuccess>
      <!-- Staging leg: move.xml with goal="{staging_pose}"; see move.xml for the why -->
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateToStaging">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathToPose">
              <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                <Inverter>
                  <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                </Inverter>
                <!-- Judged by Octo3D on its dynamic overlay (see move.xml) -->
                <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100" blocked_time="10.0"/>
              </ReactiveSequence>
              <Sequence name="PlanToStaging">
                <!-- {goal} is the charger's tag: stage in front of it, facing it -->
                <ComputeStagingPose dock_pose="{goal}" distance="1.3" facing_landmark="true" staging_pose="{staging_pose}"/>
                <ComputePathToPose goal="{staging_pose}" path="{path}" planner_id="Octo3D"/>
              </Sequence>
            </Fallback>
            <ForceSuccess>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-Context" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay"/>
            </ForceSuccess>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="4" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ForceSuccess>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
          </ForceSuccess>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>

      <!-- The tag must be in view before the gait is switched for it (header) -->
      <DetectLandmark landmark_id="charger_a" min_consecutive="3" timeout="5000"/>

      <!-- MPC half: restore RL on any failure after the switch was attempted -->
      <Fallback name="ChargeInMpc">
        <Sequence name="SwitchAndApproach">
          <SetBlackboard output_key="nav_status_msg" value="Switching the gait to MPC for charging"/>
          <SetMotionKey key="5" server_timeout="500"/>
          <!-- Confirm the controller reports MPC (mode data[1] == 5), then let it settle (dock.xml) -->
          <WaitForMotionState state="5" timeout="5000"/>
          <Wait msec="3000"/>
          <Parallel name="ApproachOnTheTag" success_threshold="1" failure_threshold="1">
            <PublishLandmark landmark_id="charger_a" min_consecutive="3" topic="tracked_landmark" sensor_timeout="5000"/>
            <TrackPose pose_topic="tracked_landmark" offset_x="0.54" offset_y="0.0" offset_yaw="3.141592653589793" controller_id="Charging" goal_checker_id="charge_goal_checker" filter_window="1.0"/>
          </Parallel>
        </Sequence>
        <ForceFailure>
          <SetMotionKey name="RestoreRL" key="1" server_timeout="500"/>
        </ForceFailure>
      </Fallback>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
